# ======================================
# Environment Variables for Bolt.diy
# ======================================
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in your API keys
# See README.md for setup instructions

# ======================================
# DEFAULT LLM PROVIDER & SIGNUP CREDITS
# ======================================
# These are the IN-CODE defaults, repeated here only as documentation. A deploy with no .env and no
# SSM property gets exactly this: KIE + claude-sonnet-5 + a 1000-credit signup grant, with the paid
# paid rungs of the ladder at their own in-code defaults (Premium claude-opus-5 @ 1200,
# Platinum claude-fable-5 @ 2000).
#   LLM_PROVIDER          -> agent/config.ts  DEFAULT_PLATFORM_PROVIDER
#   the Standard model    -> utils/constants.ts DEFAULT_MODEL ('claude-sonnet-5')
#   the paid rung         -> billing/model-tiers.ts DEFAULT_PREMIUM_*
#   ALL prices            -> the Marketplace price list (billing/baked-market-prices.ts, updated from
#                            Settings → Admin → Marketplace prices — NEVER from env vars)
#   SIGNUP_GRANT_CREDITS  -> billing/rates.ts  getBillingConfig() fallback (1000)

# ======================================
# DEFAULT LLM PROVIDER & DEFAULT MODEL
# ======================================
LLM_MODEL=claude-opus-5
LLM_PROVIDER=KIE
SIGNUP_GRANT_CREDITS=1000
ENABLE_EXTENDED_MODELS=true

# ======================================
# THE MODEL TIER LADDER (§4.6.1a)
# Standard (LLM_MODEL, above) · Premium
#
# 🔴 PLATINUM WAS RETIRED 2026-08-14 (owner). The ladder is two rungs: Standard
# for development, Premium if you want to go higher. The retired rung's model
# moved DOWN into Premium rather than being dropped, so Standard runs Opus 5 and
# Premium runs Fable 5.
#
# ⚠️ Standard is OPUS, not Sonnet, and that is a reliability decision rather than
# a taste one: Sonnet could not reliably finish a game build. Measured over 30
# generations it failed 5 of 19 with output already billed, and its successes
# leaned on the rescue machinery (forced-continuation, unproductive-rescue,
# creation-completeness; 6-19 steps) where Opus completed 4 of 4 in 3-6 steps.
#
# ⚠️ Those counts UNDERSTATE it: "completed" means the TURN finished, not that
# the game runs. Sonnet builds that settled successfully were observed shipping
# games that freeze on load, where Opus on the same prompt produced a working
# one. Nothing currently measures whether the delivered game starts.
# Sonnet is still fully supported and priced — it is ENHANCE_PROMPT_MODEL below,
# and the right tool for light work. It is just not the model that builds games.
#
# ENABLE_EXTENDED_MODELS (above) is the master switch for EVERY paid rung —
# currently exactly one.
#   true  (default) — the ladder is offered; the pill opens a picker.
#   false           — Standard ONLY. The server sends one rung, so the picker
#                     never opens, a request body naming a paid rung runs
#                     Standard, and the settings below are ignored entirely.
#
# Set it false when the provider is only serving one model reliably — then point
# LLM_MODEL at that model and everyone runs it. It is on ONLY for the exact
# string "true"; "1"/"yes"/"TRUE" are off.
#
# ⚠️ The name was NOT changed back when Platinum was retired, and that is
# deliberate. It was ENABLE_PREMIUM_MODEL from 2026-08-08 to 2026-08-10 and back
# again; each rename of a default-ON flag is a silent money bug waiting to
# happen — a deploy that had switched the OLD key off stops having it read on
# upgrade, falls through to the default, and starts serving the paid rung to
# everyone. "EXTENDED_MODELS" reads perfectly well as "the models beyond
# Standard", a set allowed to have one member. Renaming it again needs a reason
# about BEHAVIOUR, not about number agreement.
#
# ⚠️ RETIRED AND REFUSED IF SET: PLATINUM_MODEL, PLATINUM_MINIMUM_CREDITS,
# ENABLE_PLATINUM_MODEL (the rung retired 2026-08-14), ENABLE_PREMIUM_MODEL (the
# master switch's name for two days in Aug 2026), SUPERMAX_MODEL and
# SUPERMAX_MINIMUM_CREDITS. None is adopted silently: a value chosen against a
# different rung's threshold and price list must be reviewed before it serves a
# paid class. PLATINUM_MODEL would happen to be right today — Premium inherited
# exactly that model — which is precisely why adopting it is the wrong habit.
# ======================================
PREMIUM_MODEL=claude-fable-5
PREMIUM_MINIMUM_CREDITS=1500

# ======================================
# ENHANCE PROMPT MODEL (the ✨ button)
#
# Which model rewrites a user's prompt. Prompt enhancement reads no project
# files, calls no tools, keeps no history and shares no cached prefix — it turns
# <=10k characters of English into better English.
#
# SHIPPED DEFAULT: claude-sonnet-5 (owner decision, 2026-08-11) — the same model
# the platform builds with, chosen for OUTPUT QUALITY. An enhanced prompt is the
# input to the most expensive turn in the product, so a worse rewrite is not
# saved money, it is a worse game built at full price. Sonnet 5 also supports
# adaptive thinking, which Haiku 4.5 does NOT (probed: api.anthropic.com returns
# `400 adaptive thinking is not supported on this model`).
#
# THE CHEAPER OPTION IS STILL HERE, and the arithmetic is unchanged: on Anthropic
# Sonnet 5 is a flat 3x Haiku 4.5 on BOTH input and output ($3/$15 vs $1/$5); on
# Comet it is exactly 2x ($1.60/$8.00 vs $0.80/$4.00). Measured, one enhancement:
# 295 in / 335 out = 1 credit on Haiku, 2 on Sonnet. Set it to a haiku id if the
# volume ever makes that matter — but read the per-gateway note below first,
# because haiku is exactly the model whose id differs per gateway.
#
# ⚠️ With LLM_MODEL=claude-opus-5 this value now DIFFERS from the platform model, so
# setting it and unsetting it do the same thing today. It is written explicitly
# anyway: "the enhancer runs Sonnet 5" should be a decision on the page, not a
# coincidence you have to derive from another variable.
#
# Unset = the platform model (LLM_MODEL above), i.e. exactly what shipped before
# this knob existed. It is a SELECTOR like every other model variable: only
# accepted if the active price list prices it in its own right, otherwise billing
# refuses loudly rather than falling back — an unpriced model bills at the most
# EXPENSIVE row we know, which would invert the one setting whose whole purpose
# is to spend less.
#
# ENABLE_EXTENDED_MODELS does NOT gate this. That flag stops users opting into
# the expensive paid rung; this is an operator setting that spends less, and the
# cost-conscious deploy must not be the one that cannot have a cheap enhancer.
#
# ONE MODEL, DIFFERENT IDS PER GATEWAY (2026-08-11) — why the per-gateway keys exist even though the
# shipped default does not need them. Haiku 4.5 is `claude-haiku-4-5` on KIE and Anthropic and ONLY
# `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` on Comet — the bare id is a hard 400 there, and the dated one reports
# `code: "claude-haiku-4-5"`, so it looks like one model and is two strings. A single value therefore
# cannot be right on more than one gateway, and once AUTO_MODEL_SELECT can move the gateway per
# request, "right today" stops being something you can rely on.
#
# `claude-sonnet-5` happens to be the SAME id on all three gateways, so the default below is one line.
# Do not read that as the keys being unnecessary — it is the property of that particular id.
#
# Precedence, per request, against the gateway actually SELECTED:
#     <PROVIDER>_ENHANCE_PROMPT_MODEL  ->  ENHANCE_PROMPT_MODEL  ->  that gateway's platform model
#
# The generic value below still works and is still REFUSED (a loud 503 on the enhance button, naming
# the per-gateway variable as the fix) when the selected gateway cannot price it — never silently
# swapped for the platform model, which would spend several times more than the operator asked for.
# ======================================
ENHANCE_PROMPT_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
# KIE_ENHANCE_PROMPT_MODEL / COMET_ENHANCE_PROMPT_MODEL / ANTHROPIC_ENHANCE_PROMPT_MODEL — left
# unassigned as PROSE, never as commented assignments: a line that opens `VAR=value` becomes a real
# assignment the moment somebody strips the `#`. They override the value above per gateway, and the
# case they exist for is haiku:
#     KIE_ENHANCE_PROMPT_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5
#     COMET_ENHANCE_PROMPT_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001   <- the dated id; the bare one 400s here

# ======================================
# GITHUB & GITLAB OAUTH CONFIGURATION
# ======================================
# https://github.com/settings/developers
# https://gitlab.com/-/user_settings/applications
# ======================================
# http://localhost:5173/api/git/callback/github 
# http://localhost:5173/api/git/callback/gitlab
# ======================================
# openssl rand -base64 32; openssl rand -base64 32
# ======================================
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
GITLAB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GITLAB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
GIT_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
GIT_OAUTH_STATE_SECRET=

# ======================================
# VM PROVIDER API KEYS
# ======================================

# Which sandbox runtime this BUILD uses. A build-time switch, not a runtime toggle.
#
# ONLY "nodepod" is accepted. "webcontainer" and "codesandbox" are DISABLED IN CODE
# (ENABLED_SANDBOX_PROVIDERS in app/lib/common/sandbox-runtime.ts): WebContainers is proprietary and
# commercially licensed, CodeSandbox bills per VM-hour, so neither may be reachable by configuration.
# Setting either here does nothing but log a refusal — re-enabling one for debugging means editing
# that list and the test pinning it. Unset or mistyped also resolves to nodepod.
#
# The VITE_ prefix is deliberate and safe HERE ONLY: the client must know which runtime to boot, and
# the runtime's NAME is not a secret. Every other CODESANDBOX_* var below stays server-only.
VITE_SANDBOX_PROVIDER=

# ⚠️ Every CODESANDBOX_* variable below is INERT while that provider is disabled. They are kept, and
# kept documented, because the provider is dormant rather than deleted (hide-don't-delete).

# CODESANDBOX SECRET API KEY — runs the USER'S generated project (SPEC §8).
# Server-only. NEVER prefix with VITE_ (Vite inlines those into the client bundle) and never
# return it in a response body; the browser gets a scoped per-sandbox session instead.
# Mint at https://codesandbox.io/t/api with all five scopes:
#   Sandbox Creation, Sandbox Read, Sandbox Edit, VM Manage, Preview Token Manage
CODESANDBOX_API_KEY=

# The template each new project forks. An ALIAS built by `csb build ./template --alias btk@starter`,
# not a raw sandbox id — an alias is a stable pointer to an immutable snapshot, so promoting a new
# starter is a config change (SPEC §4.4 pin-and-promote). Default: btk@starter
#
# ⚠️ A PROMOTED PIN OUTRANKS THIS (Settings → Admin → Sandbox template). That is the point of
# promoting one — the pin is the reviewed decision and this is the escape hatch for template
# development — but it means setting this while a pin exists changes nothing, silently. Roll the pin
# back (or clear it) if you need this variable to be what decides.
CODESANDBOX_TEMPLATE=

# VM size per project sandbox: Pico | Nano | Micro | Small | Medium | Large | XLarge.
# MEASURED both halves: a Vite dev server on the Babylon starter uses ~490MB (Pico is plenty), but
# `vite build` peaked at 1,958MB of Pico's 2,053MB — 4.6% headroom, surviving on swap. Hence Nano.
#
# 💰 THIS VARIABLE ALSO SETS THE BILLING RATE. `vmUsdPerHourForTier` derives $/hr from it (Pico
# $0.074, Nano $0.149, larger tiers ~$0.0745 per CPU-hour), so raising it is a MARGIN decision:
# at Nano every pack and plan still clears the floor (worst case 2.41x), at Micro the Pro and Studio
# packs and ALL subscription plans fall under it. Nano is the last free step up.
# Default: Nano
CODESANDBOX_VM_TIER=

# Idle seconds before the VM hibernates. This is the main cost lever — a hibernated sandbox bills
# nothing and resumes in 1-3s with its filesystem AND its running dev server intact. Max 86400.
# Default: 300
CODESANDBOX_HIBERNATION_SECONDS=

# Lifetime of a minted preview host token, in minutes. Short by design: it is a bearer credential that
# travels in an iframe URL (and so into history and proxy logs), expiry is its only mitigation, and the
# builder re-mints it automatically before it dies. Max 1440 (24h); a nonsensical value is ignored.
# Default: 60
CODESANDBOX_HOST_TOKEN_MINUTES=

# How many of ONE account's sandboxes may be running at the same time. Opening a project never
# refuses — the oldest running VM is hibernated to make room (free, and it resumes in 1-3s with its
# dev server intact). A nonsensical value is ignored. Default: 2
CODESANDBOX_MAX_RUNNING_VMS=

# ======================================
# AI PROVIDER API KEYS
# ======================================

# --- Which provider the PLATFORM buys tokens from (SPEC §4.2a) --------------------
# LLM_PROVIDER accepts Anthropic, KIE or Comet (default: KIE — see agent/config.ts. No .env file
# is required.)
#
# All three serve the SAME Claude models over the same Anthropic-native Messages API — KIE and
# Comet are passthrough gateways, not different models — so this is purely a cost/reliability
# decision. A name that is not on the list is a hard, describable error at config time, never a silent
# fallback to another provider (which would spend a key you did not choose, at a price you did not pick).
#
# COST LADDER, measured 2026-08-10 and honest in both directions: KIE is cheapest (~60-72% off
# official) but was measurably failing — batched delivery, empty thinking text, a ~30s silent-step
# kill. Comet is a uniform ~20% off official, i.e. roughly 2x KIE on every Claude model and roughly
# 20% under Anthropic-direct, and it passed every one of those failure points. Margin is unaffected
# (credits are cost-proportional), but a credit BUYS about half as much on Comet as on KIE — that
# is a positioning consequence, not a billing bug, and it is NOT to be answered by lowering
# CREDIT_MARGIN (that hides a provider cost increase inside the pricing model).
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS COUPLED TO SIGNUP_GRANT_CREDITS *AND* TO THE MODEL. Credits are cost-proportional, so the
# grant's real purchasing power moves with both. At CREDIT_MARGIN=4.0 and the 2026-07-30 defaults
# (claude-opus-5, with the creation charge at 100) a cold build turn is ~231 credits on KIE and ~576 on
# Anthropic, so a 1000 grant clears the 1.5x floor on BOTH (3.90x / 1.56x) — `grantHeadroom()` in
# billing/rates.ts is the guard and billing.spec.ts asserts it. Re-run it whenever the provider OR the
# model changes: the old 800/150 pairing measured 1.13x on Anthropic + opus-5, i.e. below the floor.
#
# ⚠️ THE STANDARD RUNG IS NOW claude-sonnet-5 (2026-07-31, §4.6.1a) — 2.73x cheaper again, so the
# headroom above is a FLOOR rather than a current reading; it is cleared by a wider margin, not a
# narrower one, which is why the grant was not re-tuned with the model. The figures in the paragraph
# above are opus-5 history: `grantHeadroom()` computes from the LIVE default and billing.spec.ts is the
# authority, so re-run it rather than reading these lines as current.
#
# ⚠️ It carries a KNOWN VENDOR RISK, shipped knowingly: KIE 500'd on 77% of claude-sonnet-5 requests
# when it was measured on 2026-07-30 (against an interleaved opus-5 control at 5%), which is why an
# earlier attempt at this switch was reverted. The mitigation is that reverting is CONFIG ONLY —
# point the LLM_MODEL assignment above at claude-opus-5 and the Standard rung moves back, with no
# rebuild and no redeploy (§4.2a). Written as prose rather than as a commented assignment on purpose:
# a second `LLM_MODEL=` line anywhere in this file is the two-writers footgun, and the duplicate-key
# pin counts commented lines for exactly that reason. Re-probe with
#   PROBE_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5 node scripts/cache-probe.mjs
# if builds start failing — it is a vendor fault and may clear, or return, without notice.
#
# To move the platform to Anthropic, change the LLM_PROVIDER assignment near the top of this file.
# This line used to be a commented `LLM_PROVIDER=Anthropic`, which was the same two-writers footgun the
# paragraph above warns about, one variable to the left: uncommenting it made a LATER line win over the
# real assignment, silently spending a key and a price the operator had not chosen. It is prose now,
# and the duplicate-key pin covers LLM_PROVIDER as well.

# --- AUTO_MODEL_SELECT: let the platform pick the gateway per request (SPEC §4.2a) ------
# Setting AUTO_MODEL_SELECT to true turns on the preference ladder. DEFAULT OFF: with it unset the
# provider is exactly LLM_PROVIDER above, byte for byte, on every generation.
#
# (Deliberately NOT written as `AUTO_MODEL_SELECT=true` even in a comment: this block's closing note
# says these are prose rather than commented assignments, and a line that OPENS with `VAR=value`
# becomes a real assignment the moment somebody strips the `#` — with the rest of the sentence
# riding along as the value.)
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. Credits are cost-proportional, so a cheaper gateway is not our margin — it is the
# user's pack going further ("any discount on the model shows up in the user's amount of credit
# power"). And a fixed LLM_PROVIDER made a vendor outage a config edit plus a redeploy, which is how
# the KIE outage was actually experienced.
#
# LLM_PROVIDER_CHAIN is the order, best rate first. Default: KIE,Comet,Anthropic — cheapest gateway
# first, full price last. Anthropic is LAST because it is the only rung that is never a discount, and
# it is PRESENT because "every gateway is down" has to degrade to an expensive turn, not to no
# product. A name that is not a platform provider is a hard error, exactly like LLM_PROVIDER: silently
# skipping a typo would shorten the ladder in a way that looks like normal operation forever.
#
# A rung is skipped when it has no API key, or when its price list cannot price the model it would run
# IN ITS OWN RIGHT. That second gate is the money one: ratesFor() falls back to a provider's MOST
# EXPENSIVE row for an unpriced model, so failing over to a gateway that does not price the model
# would run the turn and bill it at the top of that gateway's table — the cheap rung becoming the
# biggest bill in the product, silently.
#
# THE CHOICE IS MADE ONCE, AT REQUEST START, NEVER MID-STREAM (owner decision). One gateway serves a
# turn from first token to settlement, so the gateway that spent the tokens is by construction the one
# whose rates bill them.
#
# ⚠️ FLAPPING IS EXPENSIVE AND SILENT. The ~150k-token cached prefix is PER GATEWAY, so every switch
# pays a full cache write at 2x — roughly 8x one prefix before it warms again. A provider that fails
# before producing any output is skipped for PROVIDER_COOLDOWN_MS (5 minutes, agent/provider-select.ts)
# and returns to the front the moment it serves a turn. Do not shorten that: a cooldown far below a
# warm-up window costs more in cache writes than the outage costs in failures.
#
# Left as prose rather than commented assignments — a second AUTO_MODEL_SELECT= or LLM_PROVIDER_CHAIN=
# line anywhere in this file is the two-writers footgun the duplicate-key pin exists to catch.

# --- Which MODEL, and what it costs (SPEC §4.2a, §4.6) ----------------------------
# The LLM_MODEL variable (assigned once, in the provider/model block near the top) overrides the configured
# provider's default model, on any provider. It is deliberately NOT re-assigned here, even commented:
# a later line wins in a real .env, so a second assignment anywhere in this file is a live footgun.
#
# ⚠️ A model is only usable if we can BILL it. LLM_MODEL is validated against the rate tables, not
# against a list of names — because an unpriced model does NOT fail, it bills at the most expensive
# model we know of, silently and forever. On Anthropic, a new model therefore needs a row in
# MODEL_RATES (a price cannot be guessed, only looked up). On KIE, add its row to the Marketplace
# price list from the Admin panel instead — see the selector notes below.
#
# --- KIE_DEFAULT_MODEL (selector only, 2026-07-18) ---
# Names the model KIE serves by default. Its PRICE is a row in the Marketplace price list — the
# selector is only ACCEPTED if the active list prices it, otherwise billing refuses loudly with
# directions to Settings → Admin → Marketplace prices. The old KIE_*_DOLLARS vars are retired and
# REFUSED if set (a price var nothing reads is a mis-bill waiting to be believed).
#
#   KIE_DEFAULT_MODEL=gpt-5-6-sol     # works once the admin panel has promoted a list with its row
#
# Precedence: LLM_MODEL > KIE_DEFAULT_MODEL > the provider's built-in default. That is safe because
# whatever wins must be priced in its OWN right — LLM_MODEL is never billed at KIE_DEFAULT_MODEL's
# rates. After changing any of this, re-check SIGNUP_GRANT_CREDITS: a cheaper model makes the free
# grant go further, and `grantHeadroom()` in billing/rates.ts is the guard.

# --- THE MODEL TIER LADDER (§4.6.1a) ---
# Three ordered classes a credits user may choose between, cheapest first:
#
#   Standard   the platform model (LLM_MODEL above) — the default, and always available
#   Premium    PREMIUM_MODEL    unlocked at PREMIUM_MINIMUM_CREDITS
#
# Set none of them and the ladder still works, from the in-code defaults: Premium is claude-opus-5 at
# 1200 credits and Platinum is claude-fable-5 at 2000. The values ASSIGNED above are the shipping
# config (1500 / 2000). The ladder is a LIST, not a fixed set of rungs, so its length can change
# without any rule being rewritten — it has now been 2, then 3, then 2, then 3 (a `SuperMax` rung ran
# 2026-07-31 to 2026-08-08; PLATINUM took that position under a better name on 2026-08-10), and every
# one of those changes touched the table rather than the rules.
#
# ⚠️ Rungs order CAPABILITY, not price, and those are not the same on every provider: on Comet
# fable-5 ($8/$40) really is dearer than opus-5 ($4/$20), but on Anthropic-direct the fable rung
# settled 814 credits against Opus 5's 1,017. Do not reorder the ladder to "fix" that.
#
# ⚠️ Each variable is a SELECTOR, never a price. A rung's model is priced by the ACTIVE Marketplace
# price list, and a selector the list cannot price is REFUSED loudly with directions to
# Settings → Admin → Marketplace prices. The PREMIUM_*_DOLLARS vars are retired and REFUSED if set.
#
# The client sends a TIER ID (`standard` | `premium`), never a model string — that is what
# keeps §4.2a's "model choice is config, never a user choice" true while still letting a user pick a
# class. The server maps the id to that rung's model and re-checks the threshold on every generation
# (`decideModelTier`); anything unrecognised resolves DOWN to Standard, never up.
#
# The minimums PROTECT the free signup grant: 1000 granted < 1500 minimum, so a fresh account cannot
# burn its grant on an expensive model before it has bought credits or subscribed. After changing any
# of this, re-check SIGNUP_GRANT_CREDITS — `grantHeadroom()` in billing/rates.ts is the guard.
#
# ⚠️ This block deliberately RE-ASSIGNS NOTHING. Every one of these keys is set exactly once, in the
# ladder block near the top of this file. A commented `# PREMIUM_MODEL=...` here would be a second
# assignment: uncommenting it in a real .env makes the LATER line win, which is the two-writers trap
# this file has already been bitten by (SIGNUP_GRANT_CREDITS, set twice with different values). The
# duplicate-key test counts commented lines for exactly that reason.

# KIE.AI SECRET API KEY — the PLATFORM's LLM key when LLM_PROVIDER=KIE. Server-only, never VITE_.
# Get your API key from: https://kie.ai/api-key
#
# NOTE: user projects may also declare KIE_API_KEY in their own .mcp.json (the kie-image MCP server).
# That is a DIFFERENT, user-scoped secret living in the project's own .env inside the WebContainer.
# The two never meet: the MCP bridge captures env KEY NAMES only and spawns with `env: {}` (§4.14),
# so this platform key is never visible to user game code. Do not "helpfully" wire them together.
KIE_API_KEY=your_kie_api_key_here

# Anthropic Claude — the PLATFORM's LLM key when LLM_PROVIDER=Anthropic.
# Get your API key from: https://console.anthropic.com/
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your_anthropic_api_key_here

# COMET SECRET API KEY — the PLATFORM's LLM key when LLM_PROVIDER=Comet. Server-only, never VITE_.
# Get your API key from: https://api.cometapi.com/console/token
#
# Comet fronts Anthropic's NATIVE Messages API (not an OpenAI-compat shim), which is the whole
# reason it is usable here: a /v1/chat/completions endpoint cannot carry cache_control, so routing
# Claude traffic through one silently disables every cache breakpoint (§4.2.8) and costs ~3x on every
# edit turn, with identical bytes returned and nothing thrown.
#
# The same key also reaches Comet's other surfaces (chat-completions for Grok/Kimi/Qwen/GLM/DeepSeek/
# MiniMax and gpt-*, native Gemini, and the image/video routes) — one key, one account, one bill.
COMET_API_KEY=your_comet_api_key_here

# Optional — override Comet's origin (a proxy, a regional endpoint). Unset uses api.cometapi.com/v1.
#
# ⚠️ Unlike KIE_BASE_URL, which is deliberately CLAUDE-SCOPED, this repoints EVERY family: Comet serves
# all of them from one origin, so an operator setting it means all of it. Left unassigned rather than
# written as a commented COMET_BASE_URL=... line — a second assignment in a real .env makes the LATER
# line win, which is the two-writers trap this file has already been bitten by, and the duplicate-key
# pin counts commented lines for exactly that reason.

# --- Media generation (SPEC §4.16) --------------------------------------------------
#
# WHO SERVES IMAGE AND VIDEO RENDERS — its own switch, deliberately. Unset, media follows
# LLM_PROVIDER; set, it does not. Media is a separate money path (an exact price debited BEFORE any
# spend, its own refund machinery, its own price rows), so tying it to the text cutover would move
# every render to a gateway whose media surface had not been driven. Accepts KIE or Comet, validated
# like LLM_PROVIDER; it uses that provider's EXISTING key above — one key, both kinds of spend.
#
# Running on Anthropic with this unset means NO media provider (Anthropic sells no renders): the
# Media panel and the agent's generate_* tools report "not configured" rather than silently spending
# on a gateway the operator never chose. (Written that way round on purpose — a comment line whose
# first content is `LLM_PROVIDER=` counts as a second ASSIGNMENT to the duplicate-key pin, which is
# precisely the two-writers trap this file has already been bitten by twice.)
#
# ⚠️ Flipping this mid-render is SAFE: the gateway is stamped on each task record at creation, so
# renders already in flight are still polled and downloaded by the provider that started them.
#
# ⚠️ RECOMMENDED VALUE: KIE — set it EXPLICITLY, and do not leave this key unset (owner, 2026-08-11).
#
# Both gateways are serveable (Comet's media client landed in T8 and was live-driven), but KIE is the
# better media platform by a wide margin, measured from the shipped price lists:
#
#   cheapest 5s clip   KIE grok-imagine  $0.040 =  54 credits
#                      Comet veo3-fast   $0.400 = 534 credits   <- 10x, and it is Comet's FLOOR
#   Veo 3 Fast, 8s     KIE  $0.30 flat per video
#                      Comet $0.64       (per-second billing, so it worsens with length)
#   catalogue          KIE 6 image + 9 video + a cut-out pass
#                      Comet 2 image + 2 video, both video models Google Veo, no cut-out
#
# Margin is unaffected either way (credits are cost-proportional), so this is entirely about how far a
# user's credits stretch — which is why it is worth pinning rather than inheriting.
#
# 🔴 UNSET IS NOT NEUTRAL: it falls through to LLM_PROVIDER. So moving the LLM to Anthropic silently
# turns media OFF (Anthropic sells no renders), and moving it to Comet silently moves media onto the
# 10x-more-expensive video catalogue. Neither says anything; the bill or the capability just changes.
#
# ⚠️ Whatever is set, a chat turn never fails over it (a degraded capability reports off, never on) and
# no render ever runs on the other gateway's key.
#
# Left unassigned rather than written as a commented MEDIA_PROVIDER=... line — a second assignment in
# a real .env makes the LATER line win. That trap is pinned for this key in `media-config.spec.ts`
# (at most one assignment, commented lines counted); the ladder's own pin in `model-tiers.spec.ts` is
# key-scoped and does NOT cover it.

# BROWSERPOD SECRET API KEY
# Get your API key from: https://browserpod.io/
BROWSERPOD_API_KEY=

# --- Agent web search (SPEC §4.2 — the `web_search` research tool) ------------------
# All OPTIONAL. With NONE set, search falls back to a NO-KEY DuckDuckGo scrape, which is best-effort:
# DuckDuckGo rate-limits/challenges server-side scrapes, so it works from some IPs and returns nothing
# from others. Set ONE of these to make research reliable. (KIE has no search API.)
# Precedence when several are set: SerpApi > Brave > SearXNG > DuckDuckGo.
#
# SerpApi (RECOMMENDED) — reliable Google results; has a FREE starter tier (upgradeable).
# Get a key from: https://serpapi.com/
# SERPAPI_API_KEY=your_serpapi_key_here
#
# Brave Search API — reliable, PAID (no free tier).
# Get a key from: https://brave.com/search/api/
# BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY=your_brave_search_key_here
#
# SearXNG — point at a self-hosted or trusted instance's base URL (no per-query cost).
# SEARXNG_URL=https://searxng.example.com

# --- Thinking (SPEC §4.2a, spec/anthropic-models.md §3.4-§3.6) --------------------
# Both are OPTIONAL. The defaults below are what ships; you only set these to override.
#
# THINKING_MODE=adaptive|disabled  (default: adaptive)
#   `disabled` is a SPEED lever, not a cost one — it buys latency with intelligence.
#   Flipping it invalidates the prompt cache once. Fable 5 ignores it (it always thinks).
#
# THINKING_EFFORT=medium|high|xhigh|max  (default: medium)
#   How hard the model works before answering. Thinking is billed as OUTPUT tokens, so this
#   is the single biggest line on the bill. The API's own default is `high` — we send `medium`.
#
#   `low` IS NOT ACCEPTED. It is not a cheap tier, it is a wrong one: on a real edit it wrote
#   to a read-only project zone (src/routing/router.tsx) and rewrote whole files instead of
#   patching them. Setting it here gets clamped back up to `medium` with a warning.
#
#   Repair turns escalate automatically (high, then xhigh) regardless of what you set.
# THINKING_MODE=adaptive
# THINKING_EFFORT=medium

